// **Several type variables** in one polymorphic procedure (ADR-0083): `pair :: (a: $A, b: $B)`. W5's
// third sub-wave, which lifts sub-wave 2's one-variable restriction (the E0268 that said "more than one
// `$T` is not instantiable yet").
//
// This is the generalisation of the machinery sub-wave 2 built, not a new one: inference, the structural
// key, the binding map and the clone were all plural by nature and had been collapsed to the
// single-variable case. Each variable is inferred from the first argument whose parameter is *directly*
// `$Var`; the structural key is the **tuple** of all bound types (ADR-0005, ADR-0083 §1), so
// `pick(s64, bool)` and `pick(s64, s64)` are distinct instantiations.
//
// What is checked here:
//
//   * two variables of **different** types (`pick_first` with `Point` and `s64`), where a one-variable
//     model could not express the pair;
//   * two variables both used, returning one (`pick_second`), so both bindings are load-bearing;
//   * one variable used across several parameters *and* a second variable — one `$A` shared, one `$B`
//     distinct (`combine`), which is ADR-0083's "several variables, one used repeatedly";
//   * the single-variable form still works (`id`), since the generalisation is a superset.
//
// Six assertions summing to 63; both engines must agree, and the native one materialises a distinct
// function per distinct *tuple* of bound types.
#import "Basic";

/// Two variables, returns the first.
pick_first :: (a: $A, b: $B) -> A {
    return a;
}

/// Two variables, returns the second — so `B`'s binding is what the result reads.
pick_second :: (a: $A, b: $B) -> B {
    return b;
}

/// `$A` used twice and a distinct `$B`: one variable repeated, one independent (ADR-0083 §3).
combine :: (a: $A, b: A, tag: $B) -> A {
    return a + b;
}

/// The single-variable form, unchanged — the generalisation is a superset.
id :: (x: $T) -> T {
    return x;
}

/// A struct so a variable can bind to something other than an integer.
Point :: struct {
    x: s64;
}

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // A = Point, B = s64 — genuinely different types, which one variable could not express.
    p: Point;
    p.x = 1;
    r := pick_first(p, 99);
    if r.x == 1 {
        n = n + 1;
    }

    // A = s64, B = Point — returns the second, so B's binding drives the result type.
    q: Point;
    q.x = 2;
    s := pick_second(40, q);
    if s.x == 2 {
        n = n + 2;
    }

    // `$A` = s64 twice, `$B` = bool once: `combine` sums the two `A`s and ignores the tag.
    if combine(40, 2, true) == 42 {
        n = n + 4;
    }

    // A distinct tuple — `$A` = s64, `$B` = s64 — is a *different* instantiation from the one above, even
    // though `combine` was already instantiated with a `bool` tag.
    if combine(10, 6, 0) == 16 {
        n = n + 8;
    }

    // The single-variable form, still working.
    if id(16) == 16 {
        n = n + 16;
    }

    // `pick_first` again at the same types as the first call — one instantiation serves both (ADR-0005).
    t: Point;
    t.x = 32;
    u := pick_first(t, 7);
    if u.x == 32 {
        n = n + 32;
    }

    // Every assertion: 63.
    if n == 63 {
        exit(0);
    }
    exit(1);
}
